% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 5, section 5.6 ("Innocent-unless-proven-guilty").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% Default reasoning with non-monotonic defeat: a person accused of a crime is
% innocent by default, UNLESS it can be shown the person committed the crime
% (negation as failure: "it is not the case that"). Being witnessed committing
% the act defeats the default. The crime is modelled as a single constant term.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a person* is accused of *a crime*.
*a person* committed *a crime*.
*a first person* witnessed *a second person* commit *a crime*.
*a person* is innocent of *a crime*.

the knowledge base innocent until guilty includes:

a person committed a crime
    if a second person witnessed the person commit the crime.

a person is innocent of a crime
    if the person is accused of the crime
    and it is not the case that
        the person committed the crime.

scenario accused is:
    bob is accused of robbing_the_bank.
    innocence expects answers ["bob is innocent of robbing the bank"].

scenario witnessed is:
    bob is accused of robbing_the_bank.
    john witnessed bob commit robbing_the_bank.
    innocence expects answers [].

query innocence is:
    which person is innocent of which crime.
